{"step_index":858,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:29:08Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:29:08+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:29:08+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean`\nTotal Lines: 537\nTotal Bytes: 27292\nShowing lines 1 to 537\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: /-\n2: GraphInvariantsComputable.lean — a *computable* mirror of the invariants in\n3: `GraphInvariants.lean`, so that a claim about one explicit finite graph can be\n4: settled by `decide` rather than by a hand proof.\n5: \n6: Why this file exists\n7: --------------------\n8: `GraphInvariants.lean` defines the invariants the way a mathematician states\n9: them: `dominationNumber G = sInf { n | ∃ s, s.card = n ∧ G.IsDominatingSet s }`.\n10: That is the right *specification*, but `sInf` over a `Set ℕ` (under\n11: `open scoped Classical`) is **noncomputable**, so the kernel cannot evaluate it\n12: on a concrete graph — measured directly, `decide` succeeds on `order`, `size`\n13: and `minDegree`, and fails on every `sInf`/`sSup` invariant (domination,\n14: annihilation, the zero-forcing family, and mathlib's own `indepNum`/`cliqueNum`).\n15: \n16: That blocks the cheapest source of formal results we have. The refutation engine\n17: kills thousands of candidates and knows exactly which graph kills each one; each\n18: such pair is a `¬∀ …` theorem whose proof is \"evaluate both sides on this graph\"\n19: — but only if the invariants actually evaluate.\n20: \n21: The definitions here are the same mathematical objects expressed as a minimum\n22: over an explicitly enumerated `Finset`, which the kernel *can* reduce. They are\n23: deliberately kept in their own namespace: nothing in the existing proof pipeline\n24: changes, and `GraphInvariants.lean` remains the specification of record.\n25: -/\n26: import Mathlib\n27: \n<truncated 26376 bytes>\n`SimpleGraph.dist` is defined\n502: through `sInf` over walk lengths), so it is reconstructed as reachability. -/\n503: def ballWithin (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ → V → Finset V\n504:   | 0,       v => {v}\n505:   | (k + 1), v =>\n506:       let B := ballWithin G k v\n507:       B ∪ B.biUnion (fun u => univ.filter (fun w => G.Adj u w))\n508: \n509: /-- Eccentricity of `v`: least `k` with every vertex within `k` steps. -/\n510: def eccentricity (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (v : V) : ℕ :=\n511:   minUpTo (Fintype.card V) (fun k => ballWithin G k v = univ)\n512: \n513: def radius (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : ℕ :=\n514:   ((univ.image (eccentricity G)).min).getD 0\n515: \n516: /-- Undefined on a disconnected graph, where some vertex never reaches all of\n517: `univ` and `minUpTo` would fall back to `n`. networkx raises there. -/\n518: def HasRadius (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] : Prop :=\n519:   ∀ v : V, ballWithin G (Fintype.card V) v = univ\n520: \n521: instance (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n522:     Decidable (HasRadius G) := by unfold HasRadius; infer_instance\n523: \n524: /- ── graph construction from an explicit edge list ────────────────────────── -/\n525: \n526: /-- The graph on `Fin n` with the given edge list, symmetrised and loop-free.\n527: This is the shape the disproof exporter emits for a refuting witness.\n528: \n529: `SimpleGraph.fromRel` supplies the symmetry and irreflexivity proofs, so this\n530: stays a definition rather than a bundle of obligations that would have to be\n531: re-discharged (and re-adjusted across mathlib versions) in generated code. It is\n532: an `abbrev` so that `DecidableRel` synthesis can see through it at the use site. -/\n533: abbrev ofEdges (n : ℕ) (es : List (Fin n × Fin n)) : SimpleGraph (Fin n) :=\n534:   SimpleGraph.fromRel (fun u v => (u, v) ∈ es)\n535: \n536: end GraphCalc\n537: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
