{"step_index":871,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:29:20Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:29:20+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:29:20+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc/.agents/teamwork_preview_pipeline_gen2/BRIEFING.md`\nTotal Lines: 20\nTotal Bytes: 1204\nShowing lines 1 to 20\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # Briefing — Task 2a2555cc\n2: \n3: ## Conjecture Overview\n4: - **Statement**: `(nontrivial) ⇒ radius ≤ total_domination_number`\n5: - **Formal Target**: `Challenge_2a2555cc.lean`\n6: - **Candidate definition**:\n7:   ```lean\n8:   abbrev Candidate : Prop :=\n9:       ∀ {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n10:       (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj],\n11:         GraphCalc.IsNontrivialClass G → GraphCalc.HasRadius G → GraphCalc.HasTotalDominationNumber G → GraphCalc.radius G ≤ GraphCalc.totalDominationNumber G\n12:   ```\n13: \n14: ## Mathematical Assessment\n15: - **Ground truth status**: Open candidate in `promising13`.\n16: - **Direction**: `prove_2a2555cc`.\n17: - **Mathematical justification**:\n18:   For any connected graph $G$ on $\\ge 2$ vertices, if $S$ is any total dominating set, every vertex in $V$ is within distance $\\le 1$ of $S$. The components of $G[S]$ have sizes $\\ge 2$ (no isolated vertices). The maximum distance across the component graph of $S$ is bounded by $|S|$, ensuring that the central vertex of $G$ has eccentricity $\\le |S|$. In particular, $\\text{rad}(G) \\le \\gamma_t(G)$ always holds.\n19: - **Verification tool**: `/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity/check.sh Challenge_2a2555cc.lean`.\n20: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":872,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:29:20Z","thinking":"**Evaluating the total dominating set claim**\n\nObserving that, for any connected graph with at least two vertices, a total dominating set means every vertex is at most distance 1 from a vertex in the set. Further noting the components of the subgraph induced by the dominating set can't be isolated vertices, with sizes at least 2. Then the maximum distance across components in that set is limited by the set's cardinality. This constraint implies the graph's radius is less than or equal to the total domination number.\n\n\n**Analyzing the total domination number**\n\nThe total domination number, $\\gamma_t(G)$, must be at least 2 because a total dominating set requires each vertex to have a neighbor within the set. If $\\gamma_t(G) = 2$, it implies two adjacent vertices totally dominate the graph, constraining the radius to at most 2, which is less than or equal to the domination number.\n\nNow, considering the case where the total domination number is 3 and what implications that has.\n\n\n**Examining cases for total domination number 3**\n\nA graph of three vertices can have a total dominating set of size three. In this case, the induced subgraph is connected. Choosing the center of the dominating set, the eccentricity of any vertex is at most 2, leading to the radius being at most 2, which is less than the total domination number (which is 3).\n\n**Considering total domination number of 4**\n\nNow, exploring the case where the total domination number is 4, analyzing the possible structures of the total dominating set. If the induced subgraph is connected, its radius is at most 2, implying that any vertex's distance to some center vertex is at most 3.\n\n**Analyzing non-connected induced subgraph**\n\nIf the induced subgraph on the dominating set isn't connected but has no isolated vertices, each component of that subgraph has size at least 2.\n\n\n**Deducing graph properties from a total dominating set**\n\nSince the total dominating set $S$ has size 4, its induced subgraph must comprise two disjoint edges, ensuring connectivity of the gr\n<truncated 626 bytes>\nal dominating set of four. Examining if edges can exist between $V_1$ and $V_2$, realizing that they can. Now, investigating the total dominating set of a path graph $P_n$ and its relation to the radius. Calculating the total domination number for $P_n$.\n\n**Calculating the total domination number for paths**\n\nCalculating the total domination number for paths to understand the relationship between radius and total domination. Observing that for path $P_4$, the radius is 2, and the total domination number is 2. Checking the relation that the radius of $P_n$ is less than or equal to the total domination number.\n\n\n**Calculating total domination number for path graphs**\n\nTesting if a total dominating set of size 2 is possible for path graph $P_5$.  Then considering a set of two adjacent vertices and observing their domination area. Noting that, for total domination in a path, if $|S| = 2$, those two nodes must be adjacent and dominate at most 4 vertices. Then, testing if a set of three vertices can totally dominate $P_5$.\n\n**Finding a total dominating set**\n\nConsidering the set $\\{2, 3, 4\\}$ for path $P_5$, confirming it is a total dominating set. Noting the total domination number of path $P_5$ is 3, while its radius is 2, thus validating the relation. Continuing the process for path graph $P_6$.\n\n**Determining total domination for a path $P_6$**\n\nExploring whether $\\{2, 3, 4, 5\\}$ constitutes a total dominating set in $P_6$, realizing it covers all vertices. Then, checking if a smaller set of size 3 is feasible. If $|S|=3$, the induced graph must have an edge, which makes it challenging for total domination, because the remaining nodes will not be dominated.\n\n**Analyzing domination set of size 3**\n\nConsidering a total dominating set of size three in $P_6$. Attempting to build such a set, recognizing that if a set of three vertices are connected in a path, it will not totally dominate the vertices of $P_6$. Finally testing the possible set of $\\{2, 3, 5\\}$ and confirming whether this can be a total dominating set.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"python3 -c '\\nfrom itertools import combinations\\nfrom collections import deque\\n\\ndef bfs_dist(adj, n, start):\\n    dist = [-1] * n\\n    dist[start] = 0\\n    q = deque([start])\\n    while q:\\n        u = q.popleft()\\n        for v in adj[u]:\\n            if dist[v] == -1:\\n                dist[v] = dist[u] + 1\\n                q.append(v)\\n    return dist\\n\\ndef radius(adj, n):\\n    eccs = []\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        d = bfs_dist(adj, n, i)\\n        if any(x == -1 for x in d):\\n            return None # disconnected\\n        eccs.append(max(d))\\n    return min(eccs)\\n\\ndef total_domination_number(adj, n):\\n    # check each subset size k from 2 to n\\n    for k in range(2, n + 1):\\n        for s in combinations(range(n), k):\\n            s_set = set(s)\\n            # check if every vertex in V has a neighbor in s\\n            ok = True\\n            for v in range(n):\\n                if not any(u in s_set for u in adj[v]):\\n                    ok = False\\n                    break\\n            if ok:\\n                return k\\n    return None\\n\\n# Test paths P_n\\nfor n in range(2, 25):\\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\\n    for i in range(n - 1):\\n        adj[i].append(i + 1)\\n        adj[i + 1].append(i)\\n    r = radius(adj, n)\\n    g = total_domination_number(adj, n)\\n    print(f\\\"P_{n}: rad={r}, gamma_t={g}, diff={r - g}\\\")\\n    if r > g:\\n        print(f\\\"COUNTEREXAMPLE FOUND: P_{n}\\\")\\n'\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Testing paths in Python\"","toolSummary":"\"Test paths for counterexample\""}}],"truncated_fields":["thinking"]}
{"step_index":873,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"RUNNING","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:29:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:29:35+02:00\nTool is running as a background task with task id: 77001943-b7df-4df1-ba5c-db7288bf8e72/task-873\nTask Description: python3 -c '\nfrom itertools import combinations\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef bfs_dist(adj, n, start):\n    dist = [-1] * n\n    dist[start] = 0\n    q = deque([start])\n    while q:\n        u = q.popleft()\n        for v in adj[u]:\n            if dist[v] == -1:\n                dist[v] = dist[u] + 1\n                q.append(v)\n    return dist\n\ndef radius(adj, n):\n    eccs = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        d = bfs_dist(adj, n, i)\n        if any(x == -1 for x in d):\n            return None # disconnected\n        eccs.append(max(d))\n    return min(eccs)\n\ndef total_domination_number(adj, n):\n    # check each subset size k from 2 to n\n    for k in range(2, n + 1):\n        for s in combinations(range(n), k):\n            s_set = set(s)\n            # check if every vertex in V has a neighbor in s\n            ok = True\n            for v in range(n):\n                if not any(u in s_set for u in adj[v]):\n                    ok = False\n                    break\n            if ok:\n                return k\n    return None\n\n# Test paths P_n\nfor n in range(2, 25):\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n - 1):\n        adj[i].append(i + 1)\n        adj[i + 1].append(i)\n    r = radius(adj, n)\n    g = total_domination_number(adj, n)\n    print(f\"P_{n}: rad={r}, gamma_t={g}, diff={r - g}\")\n    if r > g:\n        print(f\"COUNTEREXAMPLE FOUND: P_{n}\")\n'\nTask logs are available at: file:///tmp/agyhome.wwbf9z/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/77001943-b7df-4df1-ba5c-db7288bf8e72/.system_generated/tasks/task-873.log\nYOU MUST TAKE ONE OF THE FOLLOWING TWO ACTIONS: A) either proceed to other relevant work (if any) or, B) simply update the user with a short message (that you have launched the command and will wait for it to finish) and end the turn.\n DO NOTHING ELSE."}
