{"step_index":925,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:31:12Z","thinking":"**Analyzing the theorem statement**\n\nThe statement involves a universally quantified proposition over types with a finite cardinality and decidable equality, considering a simple graph with decidable adjacency. It defines a `Candidate` proposition, which likely signifies a property or condition on the graph. The term `GraphCalc.Is` must be understood in the context of the defined environment. The first goal is to unpack this.\n\n\n**Defining and connecting graph metrics**\n\nConsidering the definitions, graph radius is the minimum eccentricity over all vertices.  Total domination number is the smallest cardinality of a total dominating set. The task reduces to showing that at least one vertex's eccentricity is less than or equal to the total domination number. This is the correct path forward to proving the radius is less than or equal to the total domination number.\n\n\n**Connecting total domination to radius**\n\nThe minimum cardinality of a total dominating set exists and equals the total domination number.  Any vertex in the graph must have a neighbor within any minimal total dominating set.  The goal is now to establish that the radius is less than or equal to the cardinality of a minimum total dominating set.\n\nThe radius of the graph is bounded above by the cardinality of a minimal total dominating set, confirmed through mathematical reasoning.  However, the formalization of a center with bounded eccentricity in Lean appears complex.  Exploring if a simpler method or argument is possible is needed.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"python3 -c '\\nimport random\\nfrom collections import deque\\nfrom itertools import combinations\\n\\ndef bfs_dist(adj, n, start):\\n    dist = [-1] * n\\n    dist[start] = 0\\n    q = deque([start])\\n    while q:\\n        u = q.popleft()\\n        for v in adj[u]:\\n            if dist[v] == -1:\\n                dist[v] = dist[u] + 1\\n                q.append(v)\\n    return dist\\n\\ndef min_total_dominating_sets(adj, n):\\n    masks = [0] * n\\n    for u in range(n):\\n        for v in adj[u]:\\n            masks[u] |= (1 << v)\\n    target = (1 << n) - 1\\n    for k in range(2, n + 1):\\n        found = []\\n        for s in combinations(range(n), k):\\n            covered = 0\\n            for u in s:\\n                covered |= masks[u]\\n            if covered == target:\\n                found.append(s)\\n        if found:\\n            return found\\n    return []\\n\\nrandom.seed(42)\\nfor trial in range(500):\\n    n = random.randint(4, 10)\\n    p = random.uniform(0.15, 0.5)\\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(n)]\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\\n            if random.random() < p:\\n                adj[i].append(j)\\n                adj[j].append(i)\\n    # check connected\\n    d0 = bfs_dist(adj, n, 0)\\n    if any(x == -1 for x in d0):\\n        continue\\n    eccs = [max(bfs_dist(adj, n, i)) for i in range(n)]\\n    rad = min(eccs)\\n    tds = min_total_dominating_sets(adj, n)\\n    for s in tds:\\n        gamma_t = len(s)\\n        # Check: does there exist v in s with ecc(v) <= gamma_t?\\n        in_s_le = [v for v in s if eccs[v] <= gamma_t]\\n        any_le = [v for v in range(n) if eccs[v] <= gamma_t]\\n        if not any_le:\\n            print(f\\\"FAILED any_le: n={n}, rad={rad}, gamma_t={gamma_t}\\\")\\n        if not in_s_le:\\n            print(f\\\"Note: no vertex in s has ecc <= gamma_t, but exists in V: {any_le}, s={s}, eccs={eccs}\\\")\\nprint(\\\"Test completed.\\\")\\n'\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"10000","toolAction":"\"Testing if vertex in S has ecc <= gamma_t\"","toolSummary":"\"Test ecc in S\""}}]}
